{
  "image_name": "4ec019d130dcbe665c140319d2296761.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates glia–neuron signaling in the spinal dorsal horn during pain-related activation. The central idea is that an activated astrocyte communicates with both a primary afferent terminal and a dorsal horn neuron, while also interacting with an activated microglial cell. The astrocyte appears to release several mediators, including chemokines, D-serine, glutamate, and growth-related factors, which act through receptors and channels on nearby neurons and glia.\n\nA key inset highlights TLR4 signaling in the astrocyte, involving TRAF6 and pERK/pJNK, leading to release of CCL2/CXCL1 on one side and WNT3A/FGF2 on the other. In the larger network, these signals converge on the presynaptic afferent terminal, where increased pERK is linked to increased glutamate release. They also act postsynaptically on the dorsal horn neuron, which likewise shows elevated pERK, suggesting neuronal sensitization.\n\nMicroglia contribute additional inflammatory and purinergic signals, and multiple receptor types are shown across the different cell membranes, emphasizing dense bidirectional communication. Overall, the figure depicts a feed-forward inflammatory circuit in which astrocytes, microglia, and neurons amplify excitatory transmission and likely promote central sensitization.",
